## FOUND — the pieces exist separately
1. **"100 people" as a ship headline:** SpaceX Starship is planned for "up to 100"
   passengers (Wikipedia) — at ~100 t dry mass (stainless steel, 50 m) and
   1,300–1,585 t gross, as a Mars transport. No science-laboratory programme,
   no luxury volume class (its habitable volume per person is an order below
   LSV-001's 123.2 m3/person).
2. **Luxury at scale:** Orbital Assembly's Voyager Station concept — ~400
   people, estimated mass 2,418 t, volume 51,104 m3 (~128 m3/person, near
   LSV-001's per-person volume), restaurants/bars/gym/cinema — but it is a
   rotating STATION: orbit-bound, no propulsion mission, no transitions, and
   ~6.0 t of structure+systems per person against LSV-001's 1.74 t total
   per person.
3. **Aerogel near ships:** speculative designs (e.g. the SCG-HMH concept on
   Zenodo) use aerogel as INSULATION layers (aerogel-MLI) inside CNT-composite
   hulls. Aerogel as the primary structural material family - designed from
   equations with filed acceptance bands - NOT FOUND.
4. **Exotic-transit concepts exist in fringe literature** (dark-matter drive
   blueprints, wormhole-infrastructure frameworks like STWIN, "quantum slip"
   marketing pages). None prices the vessel as ONE BODY in a register; none
   carries an address-invariance bookkeeping law; none sits on a materials
   programme with preregistered bench kills. STWIN itself frames transport as
   installed infrastructure, explicitly not a vessel property.

